// str -- the string operations `id` does not have.
//
// There is no substring, no search and no comparison beyond == / !=. What there
// is instead is the flat store, and using it is not an optimisation but a
// requirement: `s = s + chr(c)` in a loop is the single most expensive thing a
// program in this language can do, because concatenation allocates and never
// frees. Building one 1920-character line per frame for 1000 frames costs
// 667 ms and 1.8 GB of retained memory; the same work through poke8 plus a
// single str_of_mem costs 1 ms and 5.8 MB.
//
// The engine therefore builds no string per frame at all. Score and timer
// readouts are drawn digit by digit (see txt_number) rather than formatted, and
// these helpers exist for load-time work: lexing, diagnostics and packaging.
//
// **Two rules about reading a string, both measured on this machine.** The
// runtime remembers the length of the *last* string charat was asked about, and
// that one-entry memo decides the cost of everything here:
//
//   * `len(s)` is a strlen every time it is evaluated, and it is NOT memoised. So
//     `while (i < len(s))` re-measures the whole string per character -- quadratic
//     over a 130 KB game source. Ask `charat(s, i) >= 0` instead: it is the same
//     end-of-string test, it is O(1), and it is what every loop here does.
//   * Alternating charat between two strings misses the memo on every call, so
//     each one pays a full strlen. 200 000 str_eqat calls against a 131 KB source
//     cost 167 ms for that reason alone, against 0 ms for the same number of
//     single-string reads. str_eqat is therefore fine for a test done once per
//     line (`#file `) and wrong inside a per-token loop -- lex_op2 compares byte
//     codes for exactly that reason.

// The substring s[i .. i+n). One allocation, no concatenation.
str_slice(string s, int i, int n) {
  word ad = alloc(n + 1);
  str_blit(s, i, n, ad);
} return string str_of_mem(ad, n);

str_blit(string s, int i, int n, word ad) {
  int j = 0;
  while (j < n) {
    poke8(ad + j, charat(s, i + j));
    j = j + 1;
  }
} return void;

// Does `txt` appear in `s` starting at i? The whole point is to answer that
// without materialising the substring, which is what makes the lexer's keyword
// tests allocation-free. The return clause reads as an assignment but `=` in an
// expression is equality, so this yields 1 only when every byte matched.
str_eqat(string s, int i, string txt) {
  int j = 0;
  while (j < len(txt) && charat(s, i + j) = charat(txt, j)) {
    j = j + 1;
  }
} return int j = len(txt);
